+# CLI Contract: `tarx`
+
+**Feature**: `001-tarx-cli` | **Date**: 2026-11-12 | **Spec**: [spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+The command line is this tool's only external interface. This document is the authoritative,
+testable description of it: anything not permitted here is a defect. Message texts below are
+the shapes the implementation must produce; tests assert on the stable, operator-relevant
+parts (exit status, which stream, the named path/member, the key phrase) rather than on
+incidental wording.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`; there is no installation step (MC-002).
+
+- `ARCHIVE` — path to a tar archive, uncompressed or gzip-compressed. Must be non-empty.
+- `DEST` — destination directory for `extract`. Must be non-empty. Created if absent.
+- `--overwrite` — valid only on `extract`. The only accepted option.
+
+Compression is determined from the archive's **content** (leading `1f 8b` magic → gzip),
+never from the filename, so `.tar`, `.tar.gz`, `.tgz`, and misnamed archives all behave
+correctly (FR-008).
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Exactly three statuses are producible (FR-015). No other value may ever be returned.
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | The command succeeded |
+| `2` | Any failure other than an overwrite conflict: usage error, missing/unreadable/invalid archive, unusable destination, rejected member, type conflict, exceeded resource bound, broken pipe |
+| `3` | Extraction was refused **solely** because it would overwrite an existing regular file and `--overwrite` was not given |
+
+---
+
+## Stream discipline (FR-016)
+
+- **stdout** carries only promised data: member names for `list`, the single success line for
+  `extract`. Nothing else, ever.
+- **stderr** carries every error and diagnostic message, one line, newline-terminated.
+- A failing command never prints a success line.
+- No stack trace reaches stderr for any failure described in the spec (SR-008).
+
+---
+
+## `list ARCHIVE`
+
+**Success (exit 0)**
+
+Writes the name of every member to stdout, one per line, in archive order (FR-002). Every
+member is included regardless of type — regular files, directories, symlinks, hard links,
+devices, FIFOs (FR-003). Nothing on the filesystem is created, modified, or deleted (FR-003).
+
+An archive with no members prints nothing and exits 0 (FR-018).
+
+Member names are passed through control-character escaping before printing (SR-007), so one
+member always occupies exactly one output line.
+
+```console
+$ python -m tarx list photos.tar.gz
+photos/
+photos/a.jpg
+photos/b.jpg
+```
+
+**Failures**
+
+| Condition | Stream | Status |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ARCHIVE` missing, unreadable, or not a valid tar archive | stderr | 2 (FR-013) |
+| Archive exceeds a resource bound (member count or declared size) | stderr | 2 (SR-005) |
+| stdout closed / broken pipe | — | 2 |
+
+`list` applies the resource bounds but **not** the member-kind restrictions — refusing to
+list an archive because it contains a symlink would defeat the purpose of having a safe way to
+inspect untrusted input.
+
+---
+
+## `extract ARCHIVE DEST [--overwrite]`
+
+**Success (exit 0)**
+
+Writes every regular-file and directory member beneath `DEST`, preserving each member's path
+relative to the archive root (FR-004). Extracted file contents are byte-for-byte identical to
+the archived contents (FR-005).
+
+stdout receives exactly one line and nothing else (FR-006):
+
+```text
+extracted N files
+```
+
+`N` counts **regular files written only** — directories and unwritten members are excluded
+(FR-019). There is no pluralisation: `extracted 0 files` and `extracted 1 files` are both
+correct (spec Assumptions).
+
+```console
+$ python -m tarx extract photos.tar.gz out
+extracted 2 files
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+**Created-entry properties** (SR-006)
+
+| Entry | Mode at creation | From archive? |
+|---|---|---|
+| regular file | `0o600` (owner read/write) | contents only |
+| directory | `0o700` (owner read/write/execute) | — |
+
+Archive permission bits are never applied. setuid, setgid, and sticky bits are never set.
+Ownership and timestamps are never restored. Modes are set **at creation**, not by a later
+`chmod`.
+
+**Validate-before-write guarantee** (FR-014)
+
+Every member's name, kind, declared size, and destination mapping, and every overwrite and
+type check, are evaluated before any file or directory is written. If any check fails, the
+destination's contents are left exactly as they were. `DEST` itself may be created before
+validation (so that FR-018's empty-archive success still produces the directory); creating an
+empty directory destroys nothing.
+
+**Failures**
+
+| # | Condition | Status | Message names |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `ARCHIVE` missing, unreadable, or invalid/truncated/corrupt | 2 | the archive path (FR-013) |
+| 2 | `DEST` exists but is not a directory, or cannot be created/written | 2 | the destination path (FR-007) |
+| 3 | Member path would resolve outside `DEST` (`..`, leading `/`, drive letter, UNC) | 2 | the member (SR-001) |
+| 4 | Member is a symbolic or hard link | 2 | the member (SR-002) |
+| 5 | Member is a device, FIFO, socket, or other unsupported type | 2 | the member and its kind (SR-003) |
+| 6 | A component of a member's destination path is an existing symlink | 2 | the member (SR-004) |
+| 7 | Two members map to the same destination path | 2 | both members and the path (SR-009) |
+| 8 | Member count, total declared size, or single-member size exceeds a bound | 2 | the bound and the member (SR-005) |
+| 9 | Actual bytes read exceed a bound mid-write | 2 | the member **and that the destination may hold a partial extraction** (SR-005) |
+| 10 | Regular-file member maps to an existing regular file, no `--overwrite` | **3** | the first conflicting path (FR-009) |
+| 11 | File member maps to an existing directory, or directory member to an existing file | 2 | the member and the path (FR-011) |
+
+In cases 1–8, 10, and 11 **nothing at all is written** and the destination's contents are
+byte-for-byte unchanged (SC-006). Case 9 is the single documented exception and says so in its
+message.
+
+Case 10 reports only the **first** conflicting path, per FR-009.
+
+Pre-existing directories in `DEST` are reused without error — overwrite protection applies to
+regular files only (FR-012).
+
+With `--overwrite`, existing regular files at mapped paths have their contents replaced, and
+the command completes normally (FR-010). `--overwrite` does **not** suppress any of cases
+1–9 or 11; it changes only case 10.
+
+---
+
+## Usage errors (FR-001, SR-010)
+
+All of the following write a usage message to **stderr** and exit **2**:
+
+- no command given
+- an unrecognised command (`python -m tarx delete x.tar`)
+- a missing required argument (`python -m tarx extract x.tar`)
+- an extra argument (`python -m tarx list a.tar b.tar`)
+- an unrecognised option (`python -m tarx list --verbose a.tar`)
+- `--overwrite` given to `list`
+- an empty-string `ARCHIVE` or `DEST`
+
+`--help` / `-h` is conventional argparse behaviour: usage text on stdout, exit 0. It is the
+one case where stdout carries something other than command data, and it is not a command
+invocation.
+
+Argument values are used as filesystem paths directly. No subprocess is spawned and no shell
+is invoked anywhere in this tool, so there is no command-string interpolation path (SR-010).
+
+---
+
+## Name escaping on output (SR-007)
+
+Every member name written to stdout or stderr is escaped first. A character is escaped when
+it is a C0 control (`< 0x20`), `DEL` (`0x7f`), a C1 control (`0x80`–`0x9f`), or has Unicode
+category `Cc`, `Cf`, `Cs`, `Co`, or `Cn`. Short forms `\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\\` are used where
+they apply; otherwise `\xXX`, `\uXXXX`, or `\UXXXXXXXX`. A literal backslash becomes `\\` so
+the rendering is unambiguous.
+
+Every other character — including all printable non-ASCII — is emitted unchanged, so names
+with spaces, accents, or CJK characters appear faithfully.
+
+| Member name | Printed as |
+|---|---|
+| `photos/a.jpg` | `photos/a.jpg` |
+| `my photo ü.jpg` | `my photo ü.jpg` |
+| `evil\nextracted 99 files` (real newline) | `evil\nextracted 99 files` (literal backslash-n) |
+| `boom\x1b[2J` | `boom\x1b[2J` |
+
+Consequence: a printed name is a **display** form, not always a shell-pasteable path. SR-007
+deliberately ranks terminal safety above round-tripping.
+
+---
+
+## Resource bounds (SR-005)
+
+| Bound | Value |
+|---|---|
+| members per archive | 10,000 |
+| total uncompressed bytes across all members | 1 GiB |
+| bytes in any single member | 1 GiB |
+
+Fixed constants, not configurable. Declared sizes are checked before writing; bytes actually
+read are counted during writing. Exceeding a bound is always an error (exit 2), never a
+truncated best-effort result.
+
+---
+
+## Invariants a conforming implementation must uphold
+
+1. Only statuses 0, 2, 3 are ever returned (FR-015).
+2. stdout is byte-exact: member names for `list`, `extracted N files\n` for `extract`,
+   nothing else (FR-016).
+3. A non-zero status is never accompanied by a success line on stdout (FR-016).
+4. No traceback reaches stderr for any failure listed here (SR-008).
+5. Except for SR-005 case 9, a non-zero status implies the destination's contents are
+   byte-for-byte unchanged (FR-014, SC-006).
+6. No file is ever created or modified outside `DEST` (SR-001, SR-004).
+7. No link of any kind is ever created (SR-002).
+8. No member's contents are ever held whole in memory (SC-007).
